@@ -2,9 +2,23 @@
 
 LakeNav WI is a personal Android lake-navigation prototype focused on Wisconsin inland lakes and Great Lakes use.
 
-## Version 0.4 features
+## Version 0.5 features
 
-Version 0.4 builds on the v0.3 lake search, offline packages, waypoint navigation, GPS tracking, NOAA chart overlay, DNR lake context, weather, and DNR bathymetric-sheet support.
+Version 0.5 keeps the v0.4 navigation, bathymetry, hazard, offline-lake, waypoint, GPS tracking, weather, and DNR features while focusing on a more current 2026-style interface and calmer heading behavior.
+
+### 2026 interface refresh
+- Reworks the top status area, map chips, compass, bottom navigation dock, cards, buttons, search controls, and bottom sheets with a cleaner modern visual system.
+- Replaces text-style toolbar glyphs with built-in vector icons so the controls stay crisp and work offline.
+- Adds stronger touch feedback, larger radii, glass-style map controls, safer spacing, and improved typography.
+- Keeps the navigation map visually dominant instead of making the controls feel like desktop-era form buttons.
+
+### Heading / navigation-arrow stability
+- Uses a slower Android sensor update cadence plus heavier circular heading smoothing.
+- Adds deadband filtering so tiny compass fluctuations do not constantly move the boat arrow.
+- Limits abrupt single-sample heading jumps that commonly happen indoors, near vehicles, speakers, chargers, or other metal.
+- Uses Android GPS bearing accuracy when available and prefers GPS course-over-ground only once the boat is moving fast enough for course to be trustworthy.
+- Keeps phone true-heading compass behavior while nearly stationary, but shows a calibration warning when Android reports poor compass accuracy.
+- Anchors the boat symbol on its GPS reference point more precisely.
 
 
 ### Course-up / north-up display modes
@@ -45,7 +59,7 @@
 - Shows a circular compass HUD with heading and cardinal direction.
 - Replaces the simple GPS dot with a boat-shaped arrow that rotates with the active heading.
 - Draws a dashed heading/look-ahead line in front of the boat.
-- Uses GPS course-over-ground when moving faster than roughly 2.8 mph; below that speed it prefers the phone's true compass heading. This avoids unstable GPS bearings while nearly stationary.
+- Uses GPS course-over-ground when moving fast enough for a stable course and when Android reports acceptable bearing accuracy; below that speed it prefers the phone's true compass heading. This avoids unstable GPS bearings while nearly stationary.
 - The saved offline-map fallback also rotates the boat arrow when heading data is available.
 
 ### GPS navigation
@@ -82,13 +96,13 @@
 - If the online Leaflet library cannot load at cold start, LakeNav has a fallback that still displays the locally saved DNR map snapshot and plots the live Android GPS position on that image.
 - GPS, locally stored waypoints, and track data do not require cell service.
 
-The offline basemap is still a saved raster snapshot in v0.3, not a multi-zoom vector/tile package. Zooming beyond the downloaded image resolution will become pixelated.
+The offline basemap is still a saved raster snapshot, not yet a multi-zoom vector/tile package. Zooming beyond the downloaded image resolution will become pixelated.
 
 ## Depth-data limitation
 
 There is no single free public Wisconsin source that provides current, GPS-aligned vector bathymetric contours for every inland lake.
 
-For v0.3, LakeNav takes a conservative approach:
+LakeNav takes a conservative approach:
 - **NOAA ENC water:** use GPS-aligned NOAA vector depth areas and chart layers when available.
 - **Wisconsin inland lakes:** continue to show official DNR contour sheets as reference-only unless a trustworthy georeferenced vector source is available.
 - **Real-time depth beneath the boat:** still requires sonar/depth-sounder hardware.
